# Phase 5 — BLE Passive RF Monitor

## CTW SDR Forensic Platform

Passive BLE advertising channel monitor and anomaly detection system. Captures raw IQ data from HackRF SDR, demodulates GFSK on BLE advertising channels 37/38/39, extracts advertising PDUs, and runs real-time anomaly detection to identify illegal RF activity including jamming, MAC flooding, address impersonation, and coordinated ghost device clusters.

**No decryption. No connection attempts. No active transmission. Passive receive only.**

## Architecture

The system is a two-stage pipeline:

```
HackRF → [ble_capture (C++)] → NDJSON pipe → [ble_analyzer (Python)] → Evidence Chain
               ↓                                       ↓
         IQ capture at 4 Msps                   Device profiles
         GFSK demodulation                      Anomaly detection (16 codes)
         BLE PDU extraction                     BLAKE3 hash-chained evidence
         Channel hopping (37/38/39)             Forensic report generation
```

**ble_capture.cpp** handles the time-critical RF path: HackRF IQ capture at 4 Msps, FM discriminator GFSK demodulation, BLE access address correlation, PDU extraction with CRC-24 validation, and channel de-whitening. Outputs structured NDJSON records to stdout.

**ble_analyzer.py** consumes the packet stream, builds per-device temporal profiles, and runs 16 anomaly detection checks covering four threat categories: jamming (JAM-001 through JAM-003), flooding (FLD-001 through FLD-003), impersonation (IMP-001 through IMP-003), and structural violations (GHO, STR, PRO codes). All findings are logged to a BLAKE3 hash-chained evidence file compatible with the SDAR forensic architecture.

## Anomaly Detection Codes

| Code    | Severity | Detection |
|---------|----------|-----------|
| JAM-001 | CRITICAL | Broadband energy present, zero decodable packets |
| JAM-002 | HIGH     | >80% corrupt packet ratio under elevated energy |
| JAM-003 | HIGH     | Single channel elevated >15 dB above other adv channels |
| FLD-001 | CRITICAL | >100 unique MAC addresses in 60-second window |
| FLD-002 | HIGH     | Device advertising below BLE spec minimum interval |
| FLD-003 | HIGH     | >10 new MAC addresses appearing in 2-second burst |
| IMP-001 | CRITICAL | Carrier frequency offset shift on same MAC (radio swap) |
| IMP-002 | MEDIUM   | Public address claims unregistered IEEE OUI |
| IMP-003 | MEDIUM   | Same address sending conflicting PDU types |
| GHO-001 | LOW      | Device seen <10 times in <5s lifespan, then vanishes |
| GHO-002 | CRITICAL | 5+ ghost devices appearing within 10-second window |
| STR-001 | MEDIUM   | RSSI exceeds expected BLE power class limits |
| STR-002 | MEDIUM   | Carrier frequency offset outside normal tolerance |
| PRO-001 | HIGH     | Reserved PDU type fields in use |
| PRO-002 | MEDIUM   | Malformed BLE AD structure in payload |
| PRO-003 | MEDIUM   | Extended advertising indicators in non-extended context |

## Build & Run

```bash
# Install dependencies (Kali/Debian/Ubuntu)
make install-deps

# Build C++ capture engine
make

# Run single-SDR capture (pipe to analyzer)
make run

# Run dual-SDR symmetric capture (Mode A peer test)
make run-dual

# Replay saved capture for re-analysis
make replay FILE=captured_data.ndjson

# Quick test with synthetic data
make test
```

## Dual-SDR Deployment (Mode A)

With both HackRF units, run symmetric capture from two spatial positions to enable triangulation correlation in post-processing. Each SDR produces its own evidence chain. Cross-reference timestamps and RSSI measurements between chains to establish signal directionality.

## Evidence Chain Format

Each evidence file is NDJSON with BLAKE3 hash chaining (SHA-256 fallback). Every record includes `_seq`, `_prev_hash`, and `_hash` fields forming an append-only tamper-evident log. Record types: `session_start`, `packet`, `anomaly`, `device_summary`, `session_stats`, `session_end`.

Compatible with SDAR forensic evidence containers and meets SWGDE/ISO 27037 chain-of-custody requirements when wrapped in EWF containers.

## Operational Context

This tool addresses the documented observation of 216+ simultaneous BLE advertisements in a residential environment with zero BLE-capable devices. The anomaly detection thresholds are calibrated for this scenario — a clean residential baseline should show near-zero unique BLE advertisers.

